We introduce a novel, piecewise constant Mumford-Shah model tailored for low-quality images, incorporating a weighted blend of the traditional Mumford-Shah model and total variation denoising. This approach achieves image segmentation and denoising simultaneously by minimizing a weighted average of the Mumford-Shah model and total variation denoising with adjustable emphasis on detail preservation. A fast and efficient algorithm using an adaptive splitting method ensures solving separate subproblems in parallel within a fixed iteration framework. Given the lack of a well-established theoretical basis for our model, we develop a strict convergence proof for our algorithm using second-order optimality conditions as a starting point. Our model's effectiveness is demonstrated through comprehensive experiments involving both artificial and real-world data, showcasing its ability to segment corrupted images with preserved details. Furthermore, comparative analyses with other algorithms are conducted to assess our model's performance. Given the promising outcomes and theoretical validation, the proposed model is potentially suitable for application in image denoising and segmentation. It is worthy to note that although our model can yield detailed denoising and segmentation results, it still operates within a piecewise constant representation. Therefore, it may not be a suitable choice for cases such as texture preservation. A possible extension of this model for applying to multidimensional data sets is also explored. Detailed implementation code for this model is made available at https://github.com/NicolasPaspereau/MuemfordShahTV/tree/master. We. 

